// D1 round 5 / H6 — the CROSS-ARCHIVE half, and the honest half.
//
// The callee `stash2` lives in libbcxa.a (tests/logos/bc_cross_archive/). It
// is the canonical door-F shape: the `B` is manufactured INSIDE the callee out
// of its own `&`-param and stored through its `&mut` out-param, so nothing at
// the call site is borrow-carrying and only the per-function borrow-FLOW
// SUMMARY can decide it. This program reads through the stored borrow BEFORE
// mutating, so it is correct under any sound analysis and must compile and RUN.
//
// ⚠ ITS MUTATE-AFTER TWIN IS NOT REGISTERED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Re-measured
// on this tree, the cross-archive leak is ADMITTED (rc=0): the archived body
// arrives as an unmaterialized Writ ExternalRef, so body_available() answers
// false and the summary is `UNAVAILABLE` — residual class (c), not the deleted
// (b). Pinning that with an `.expected` asserting a clean compile would record
// the defect as intended. The REFUSING side of the same program, with the
// callee's body in the consumer's own file, is
// fail/bc_d1r5_h6_local_body_twin; the pair states the invariant that WHERE A
// CALLEE'S BODY LIVES must be inert, and says openly that today it is not.
// See the residual list in src/compiler/borrow_flow_summary.inc.
package bc_d1r5_h6_cross_archive_admits;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use bc_cross_archive.bcxa;
fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let mut vs: Vec<B> = Vec::new();
    stash2(&c, &mut vs);
    let x: i64 = *vs.get(0u64).p;
    c.bump();
    return x + c.v;
}
